<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Motion Sickness: 2 Points to Prevent Nausea While Traveling" description="Prevent car sickness, seasickness, and airsickness with 2 powerful acupressure points: PC6 (Neiguan) and ST36 (Zusanli). Step-by-step guide with exact locations." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions/" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Motion Sickness", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-motion-sickness/" }]} datePublished="2026-09-04" dateModified="2026-09-04" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Motion Sickness: 2 Points to Prevent Nausea While Traveling</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Motion sickness can put a damper on any trip, whether you are cruising on a boat, flying in a plane, or riding in a car. The good news is that acupressure offers a natural, drug-free way to calm the stomach and prevent nausea before it starts.</p>
    <p class="mb-8">In this post, I will show you the 2 most effective acupressure points for motion sickness, how to locate them, and exactly how to press them for the best results.</p>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Acupressure Works for Motion Sickness</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Acupressure stimulates specific points on the body that help balance the nervous system and reduce the sensory conflict between your eyes, inner ear, and muscles that causes motion sickness. By applying targeted pressure, you can often prevent nausea from setting in or reduce its severity.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Point 1: PC6 (Neiguan) — The Inner Wrist Point</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">This is the most well-known and easiest point to access, making it perfect for travel.</p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2">Location</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Find the inner side of your forearm, about three finger widths below your wrist crease, between the two tendons.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2">How to Press</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>Use the thumb of your other hand.</li>
          <li>Apply firm, steady pressure.</li>
          <li>Massage in a small circular motion for 2–3 minutes.</li>
          <li>You can press both wrists simultaneously.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 italic">Pro Tip: Many travelers wear acupressure wristbands (Sea-Bands) that constantly apply pressure to this point.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Point 2: ST36 (Zusanli) — The Leg Point (Power Point)</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">This is a "master point" known for boosting overall energy and digestion, often used to strengthen the body's resistance to illness.</p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2">Location</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Sit down and measure four finger widths down from the bottom of your kneecap. The point is located one finger breadth (about half an inch) to the outside of the shinbone (tibia).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-5 mb-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2">How to Press</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>Use your thumb or the heel of your hand.</li>
          <li>Press firmly into the muscle (you should feel a slight soreness or fullness).</li>
          <li>Hold for 1 minute, then release.</li>
          <li>Repeat 2–3 times on each leg.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Use These Points During Travel</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li><strong>Preventative:</strong> Press PC6 before you even feel nauseous. Press ST36 once you are settled in your vehicle.</li>
        <li><strong>During Symptoms:</strong> If you start feeling queasy, immediately begin pressing PC6 in circular motions. Combine with slow, deep breathing (inhale through the nose, exhale through the mouth).</li>
        <li><strong>Combination:</strong> Many find the best results using both points simultaneously — one on each wrist (PC6) and one on each leg (ST36).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How early should I press PC6 before traveling?</summary>
        <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Press PC6 about 20–30 minutes before your trip begins. This gives the point time to activate and help prevent nausea before it starts. If you are wearing acupressure wristbands, put them on before you board.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I use acupressure for children?</summary>
        <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes, but use lighter pressure for children. PC6 is gentle enough for kids, and acupressure wristbands are a good option for young travelers. For children under 2, consult a pediatrician first.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">What if acupressure does not work for me?</summary>
        <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Acupressure works best as prevention. If you are already severely nauseous, it may be harder to reverse symptoms. Combine with other strategies: fresh air, looking at the horizon, staying hydrated, and avoiding heavy meals before travel.</div>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Final Thoughts</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Acupressure is a skill that improves with practice. Try these points on a short trip first to see how your body responds. Always consult with a healthcare professional if you have underlying health conditions.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Safe travels and happy pressing!</p>
    </section>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011", "song-2015"]} note={"General evidence for acupressure from published systematic reviews. See our research page for details."} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
